WEBVTT

00:00:07.007 --> 00:00:09.237
She's not just out of control,
she's out of her mind!

00:00:09.275 --> 00:00:10.970
If you will explain
what happened, Lieutenant.

00:00:11.011 --> 00:00:12.808
She hit me is what happened!

00:00:12.846 --> 00:00:14.245
Your nose is broken
in three places.

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:17.215
I'll need to reset
the ethmoid fossa.

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:18.376
Try not to move.

00:00:18.418 --> 00:00:20.909
We were having a disagreement
about the power grid.

00:00:20.954 --> 00:00:23.149
She wanted to realign
the lateral plasma conduit.

00:00:23.189 --> 00:00:26.283
I told her
that would cause an overload.

00:00:26.326 --> 00:00:28.590
As usual,
she wouldn't listen.

00:00:28.628 --> 00:00:31.392
So I told her to step aside
and let me handle it.

00:00:32.532 --> 00:00:33.965
She pushed me away
from the console.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:35.160
I pushed back.

00:00:35.201 --> 00:00:36.964
Next thing I knew,
I was lying on the deck

00:00:37.003 --> 00:00:39.164
with blood pouring down my face.

00:00:39.205 --> 00:00:40.229
Then what happened?

00:00:40.273 --> 00:00:41.240
She said, "Sorry.

00:00:41.274 --> 00:00:43.174
Maybe you should go
to Sick Bay."

00:00:43.209 --> 00:00:44.176
At least she gave
you some good advice.

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:45.177
Now...

00:00:45.211 --> 00:00:46.178
Ow!

00:00:46.212 --> 00:00:47.179
Hold still.

00:00:47.213 --> 00:00:49.113
Don't worry, Lieutenant,
I'll take care of this.

00:00:49.149 --> 00:00:51.014
You keep that woman
out of my Engine Room

00:00:51.051 --> 00:00:52.348
and everything will be fine!

00:00:52.385 --> 00:00:53.909
Ow!

00:00:55.622 --> 00:00:57.419
Where is Ms. Torres?

00:00:57.457 --> 00:00:58.924
I've confined her to quarters.

00:00:58.958 --> 00:01:00.016
I would like her moved
to the brig

00:01:00.126 --> 00:01:01.855
until formal charges
can be filed.

00:01:01.895 --> 00:01:04.523
Formal charges?
Come on, Tuvok.

00:01:04.564 --> 00:01:06.259
We're 75 years away
from the nearest court.

00:01:06.299 --> 00:01:07.266
You're really going

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:08.733
to hold her in the brig
for that long?

00:01:08.768 --> 00:01:10.099
The Captain has the authority

00:01:10.136 --> 00:01:11.569
to try Ms. Torres
here on the ship,

00:01:11.604 --> 00:01:13.697
considering the extenuating
circumstances.

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:15.298
Well, I'd like to settle this
on my own

00:01:15.341 --> 00:01:16.330
without filing charges

00:01:16.376 --> 00:01:17.638
and without involving
the Captain.

00:01:17.677 --> 00:01:19.144
She struck a fellow officer...

00:01:19.179 --> 00:01:20.669
that is a court-martial offense.

00:01:20.713 --> 00:01:22.237
She's a Maquis,
and in the Maquis,

00:01:22.282 --> 00:01:23.647
sometimes you
have to push people

00:01:23.683 --> 00:01:25.651
out of your way
to get things done.

00:01:25.685 --> 00:01:27.653
Ms. Torres is no longer
a member of the Maquis

00:01:27.687 --> 00:01:30.588
and with all due respect,
Commander, neither are you.

00:01:30.623 --> 00:01:32.488
Allowing Ms. Torres to get away

00:01:32.525 --> 00:01:34.152
with a clear violation
of regulations

00:01:34.194 --> 00:01:36.355
sets a bad example
for the rest of the crew.

00:01:36.396 --> 00:01:38.296
It makes it appear
as though you are

00:01:38.331 --> 00:01:39.593
showing favoritism
toward the Maquis.

00:01:39.632 --> 00:01:42.567
Look, Lieutenant, I don't have
to explain myself to you.

00:01:42.602 --> 00:01:44.433
I'll deal with B'Elanna
personally

00:01:44.471 --> 00:01:45.961
and then
I'll inform the Captain.

00:01:46.005 --> 00:01:47.996
You're to drop this matter.

00:01:48.041 --> 00:01:49.269
That's an order.

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:53.340
I will yield my authority
in this case to you.

00:01:53.379 --> 00:01:56.780
However, I will make a full
report in my Security Log.

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:02.578
You do that.

00:02:11.097 --> 00:02:13.065
Sir, we heard what happened.

00:02:13.099 --> 00:02:14.623
What are they going
to do with B'Elanna?

00:02:14.667 --> 00:02:17.101
I don't know yet,
but I'll handle it.

00:02:17.137 --> 00:02:18.331
We heard that Captain Janeway

00:02:18.371 --> 00:02:20.464
is going to put her in
the brig for two months.

00:02:20.507 --> 00:02:21.906
And that all the Maquis
are going

00:02:21.941 --> 00:02:24.102
to be restricted to quarters
when they're not on duty?

00:02:24.144 --> 00:02:25.133
Not true.

00:02:25.178 --> 00:02:26.873
We just want you to know,

00:02:26.913 --> 00:02:28.642
that if things do
get out of hand,

00:02:28.681 --> 00:02:29.943
we're ready to back you.

00:02:30.984 --> 00:02:31.951
What does that mean?

00:02:31.985 --> 00:02:33.282
In case you want

00:02:33.319 --> 00:02:35.287
to take control of the ship,
you have our full support.

00:02:38.224 --> 00:02:39.919
If I ever hear you
talk that way again,

00:02:39.959 --> 00:02:42.223
I'll personally throw you
in the brig for mutiny.

00:03:00.246 --> 00:03:01.213
Here.

00:03:01.247 --> 00:03:02.236
What's this?

00:03:02.282 --> 00:03:04.147
The medical report
on Lieutenant Carey.

00:03:04.184 --> 00:03:05.947
Lieutenant Carey is an idiot!

00:03:06.052 --> 00:03:07.178
When I tell you...

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:08.414
I don't want to hear it!

00:03:09.422 --> 00:03:10.616
You might be interested
to know that

00:03:10.657 --> 00:03:12.454
the impact fractures
along his cranium

00:03:12.492 --> 00:03:13.584
were pretty severe.

00:03:13.626 --> 00:03:14.854
If you'd hit him
just a little harder,

00:03:14.894 --> 00:03:16.691
you could have driven
some of those bones

00:03:16.729 --> 00:03:18.060
into his cerebellum.

00:03:18.097 --> 00:03:21.089
I didn't even come close
to hitting him that hard.

00:03:21.134 --> 00:03:22.465
So on the one side,
I'm facing a Vulcan

00:03:22.502 --> 00:03:23.833
who wants to court-martial you,

00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:25.861
and on the other,
I'm facing all the Maquis

00:03:25.905 --> 00:03:27.600
who are ready to seize
this ship over this.

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:30.541
You've turned this into
one lousy day for me, Torres.

00:03:36.182 --> 00:03:38.116
So, how long do I have
to stay in here?

00:03:38.151 --> 00:03:40.210
Rest of the trip... 75 years.

00:03:40.253 --> 00:03:42.346
I've never found
your twisted sense of humor

00:03:42.388 --> 00:03:43.787
very funny, Chakotay.

00:03:43.823 --> 00:03:46.257
Or I could send you back to work
with the understanding

00:03:46.292 --> 00:03:47.384
that you'll apologize to...

00:03:47.427 --> 00:03:49.588
Apologize?!
He was the one who...

00:03:49.629 --> 00:03:51.722
Not just a simple apology,
a personal one...

00:03:51.764 --> 00:03:54.096
over a hot cup of pejuta.

00:03:54.133 --> 00:03:55.157
Bond with the man.

00:03:55.201 --> 00:03:57.431
I am not going to bond with him.

00:03:57.470 --> 00:03:59.563
You're going to need
support from people like Carey.

00:03:59.606 --> 00:04:01.335
I don't need support
from anybody.

00:04:01.374 --> 00:04:04.172
You are if you're going to be
Chief Engineer of this ship.

00:04:05.245 --> 00:04:06.212
Right.

00:04:06.246 --> 00:04:07.372
This is not

00:04:07.413 --> 00:04:09.176
another example of my twisted
sense of humor.

00:04:12.018 --> 00:04:13.576
But Carey's next in line.

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:15.781
You're a better engineer
than he is.

00:04:20.426 --> 00:04:22.621
What does the Captain have
to say about this?

00:04:22.662 --> 00:04:24.095
She hasn't said a word...

00:04:25.865 --> 00:04:27.628
because I haven't told her yet.

00:06:22.313 --> 00:06:26.374
Captain's Log, Stardate 48439.7.

00:06:26.418 --> 00:06:27.783
As we maintain a course

00:06:27.819 --> 00:06:30.344
back to the Alpha Quadrant,
we're conducting

00:06:30.388 --> 00:06:32.913
what would normally be routine
maintenance to the ship...

00:06:32.957 --> 00:06:36.449
routine, that is,
if we had access to a starbase.

00:06:36.494 --> 00:06:39.292
Engine efficiency is down
another 14 percent.

00:06:39.330 --> 00:06:41.321
If we don't get more power
to the warp drive,

00:06:41.366 --> 00:06:43.357
we're all going to have
to get out and push.

00:06:43.401 --> 00:06:45.426
What about
alternative energy sources?

00:06:45.470 --> 00:06:47.961
Ensign Kim, have you had
any luck getting power

00:06:48.073 --> 00:06:49.438
from the holodeck reactors?

00:06:49.474 --> 00:06:50.441
Not yet.

00:06:50.475 --> 00:06:51.840
We tried hooking them
to the power grid

00:06:51.876 --> 00:06:54.572
and we ended up blowing out
half the relays.

00:06:54.612 --> 00:06:57.206
The holodeck's energy matrix,
it just isn't compatible

00:06:57.248 --> 00:06:58.374
with the other power systems.

00:06:58.416 --> 00:07:00.111
Captain, if we relocate

00:07:00.151 --> 00:07:01.448
all security personnel
to Deck 7,

00:07:01.486 --> 00:07:03.044
we can shut down power
on Deck 9

00:07:03.088 --> 00:07:04.885
and reroute it to propulsion.

00:07:04.923 --> 00:07:08.518
That would be inconvenient,
but acceptable.

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:11.393
Fine. Now let's move on
to the personnel situation.

00:07:11.429 --> 00:07:12.726
We still have...

00:07:13.832 --> 00:07:16.665
Sorry we're late,
but I wasn't informed

00:07:16.701 --> 00:07:18.760
there was a meeting
this morning.

00:07:20.038 --> 00:07:22.563
Oh, there don't seem
to be enough chairs.

00:07:22.607 --> 00:07:26.065
Neelix, this is a briefing
for the Senior Officers.

00:07:26.111 --> 00:07:27.476
I see.

00:07:27.512 --> 00:07:29.275
Well, I...

00:07:29.314 --> 00:07:32.147
I am the senior Talaxian
on board.

00:07:32.183 --> 00:07:35.516
Kes is the senior Ocampa.

00:07:35.553 --> 00:07:37.953
And I do know more
about this region of space

00:07:38.056 --> 00:07:40.388
than any other member
of the crew.

00:07:40.425 --> 00:07:43.061
We have some excellent
suggestions, Captain.

00:07:44.195 --> 00:07:45.787
Very well.

00:07:45.830 --> 00:07:48.697
You're welcome to join us,
this time.

00:07:49.467 --> 00:07:50.900
Here you are.

00:07:52.970 --> 00:07:56.167
To be honest, we could use
some excellent suggestions

00:07:56.207 --> 00:07:57.196
right now.

00:07:57.241 --> 00:07:58.606
I've been thinking
that you might be able

00:07:58.643 --> 00:08:00.372
to convert
one of your lower decks

00:08:00.411 --> 00:08:02.743
into a hydroponics bay
to grow your own food.

00:08:02.780 --> 00:08:04.873
I understand that
the replicators are down

00:08:04.916 --> 00:08:07.714
and that the emergency rations
won't hold out much longer.

00:08:07.752 --> 00:08:09.811
What about Cargo Bay 2?

00:08:09.854 --> 00:08:11.913
It was designed
for organic storage

00:08:11.989 --> 00:08:14.355
and it already has adjustable
environmental controls.

00:08:15.460 --> 00:08:16.620
When can you start?

00:08:16.661 --> 00:08:17.923
Me?

00:08:18.029 --> 00:08:19.428
It's your idea.

00:08:19.464 --> 00:08:20.556
It's your project.

00:08:20.598 --> 00:08:22.293
Right away.

00:08:22.333 --> 00:08:26.565
I can do some wonderful things
with vegetables, Captain.

00:08:26.604 --> 00:08:30.938
My feragoit goulash
is known across 12 star systems.

00:08:31.042 --> 00:08:32.475
Okay.

00:08:32.510 --> 00:08:34.478
The personnel situation.

00:08:34.512 --> 00:08:36.446
We've managed to find
a replacement

00:08:36.481 --> 00:08:37.948
for the Transporter Chief,

00:08:38.015 --> 00:08:40.210
but we still need
an Astrogation Plotter,

00:08:40.251 --> 00:08:42.879
a Chief Engineer,
medical support personnel.

00:08:42.920 --> 00:08:45.354
I've made a list
of several Maquis crew members

00:08:45.389 --> 00:08:47.220
who would make good officers.

00:08:49.560 --> 00:08:50.822
B'Elanna Torres?

00:08:50.862 --> 00:08:54.696
She was the one involved
in that incident with Mr. Carey.

00:08:54.732 --> 00:08:56.165
That's right.

00:08:56.200 --> 00:08:59.465
Just what job do you
think she's suited for?

00:08:59.504 --> 00:09:01.335
Chief Engineer.

00:09:02.373 --> 00:09:03.772
You're serious.

00:09:03.808 --> 00:09:05.435
Very.

00:09:08.713 --> 00:09:10.613
Regarding Sick Bay...

00:09:10.648 --> 00:09:13.242
we still need
a Chief Medical Officer.

00:09:13.284 --> 00:09:16.310
What about that electronic
man down in Sick Bay?

00:09:16.354 --> 00:09:18.015
It is an emergency
medical hologram

00:09:18.055 --> 00:09:19.852
and its abilities are limited.

00:09:19.891 --> 00:09:22.485
It can only operate
within the confines of Sick Bay.

00:09:22.527 --> 00:09:24.927
Not to mention
its lousy bedside manner.

00:09:24.962 --> 00:09:26.088
Perhaps we should assign

00:09:26.130 --> 00:09:27.427
somebody to train
with the hologram

00:09:27.465 --> 00:09:28.454
as a Field Medic.

00:09:29.467 --> 00:09:30.695
Good idea.

00:09:30.735 --> 00:09:34.933
Lieutenant, I understand
you studied biochemistry

00:09:35.006 --> 00:09:36.132
at the Academy?

00:09:36.174 --> 00:09:38.540
Uh, only two semesters.

00:09:38.576 --> 00:09:39.804
Close enough.

00:09:39.844 --> 00:09:42.108
You just volunteered to become
a Field Medic.

00:09:42.146 --> 00:09:44.205
Report to Sick Bay
as soon as we're finished here.

00:09:44.248 --> 00:09:45.613
But, Captain...

00:09:45.650 --> 00:09:46.742
Stations, everyone.

00:09:49.320 --> 00:09:50.617
Report!

00:09:50.655 --> 00:09:52.520
We're running into some kind
of spatial distortions.

00:09:52.557 --> 00:09:53.717
Mr. Tuvok?

00:09:53.758 --> 00:09:54.782
The distortions are emanating

00:09:54.825 --> 00:09:56.122
from a highly
localized disturbance

00:09:56.160 --> 00:09:57.889
in the space-time continuum.

00:09:57.929 --> 00:10:00.261
Distance... 20,000 kilometers
off the port bow.

00:10:00.298 --> 00:10:01.287
All stop.

00:10:03.601 --> 00:10:04.795
On screen.

00:10:08.172 --> 00:10:11.471
Gravimetric flux density
is over 2,000 percent.

00:10:11.509 --> 00:10:12.976
If I'm not mistaken,

00:10:13.077 --> 00:10:16.376
we're looking at a type-4
quantum singularity.

00:10:18.649 --> 00:10:20.708
Captain, I am receiving
an audio transmission...

00:10:20.751 --> 00:10:23.584
from within the singularity.

00:10:23.621 --> 00:10:24.883
On speakers.

00:10:24.922 --> 00:10:26.480
This is... ship

00:10:26.524 --> 00:10:27.991
...singularity...

00:10:29.293 --> 00:10:31.420
I think I've found
the source of the transmission.

00:10:38.869 --> 00:10:40.894
Does it look like any ship
you're familiar with?

00:10:40.938 --> 00:10:42.997
No.

00:10:43.040 --> 00:10:45.270
Nothing I recognize...

00:10:45.309 --> 00:10:49.302
but then it's, it's
so hard to make out.

00:10:49.347 --> 00:10:51.372
They may be trapped
in the event horizon.

00:10:51.415 --> 00:10:52.404
Open a channel.

00:10:53.784 --> 00:10:56.344
This is Captain Kathryn Janeway
of the Starship Voyager

00:10:56.387 --> 00:10:59.447
to the vessel near
the quantum singularity.

00:10:59.490 --> 00:11:00.457
Do you need help?

00:11:00.491 --> 00:11:01.685
Event horizon?

00:11:01.726 --> 00:11:04.388
A singularity is a star

00:11:04.428 --> 00:11:07.397
that's collapsed in on itself.

00:11:07.431 --> 00:11:10.867
The event horizon is
a very powerful energy field

00:11:10.901 --> 00:11:12.232
surrounding it.

00:11:12.270 --> 00:11:16.604
Why, once on a particularly
dangerous trade mission

00:11:16.641 --> 00:11:18.506
to the twin stars of Keloda,

00:11:18.542 --> 00:11:20.601
I myself was almost
trapped inside...

00:11:20.645 --> 00:11:22.636
No response to our hail,
Captain.

00:11:22.680 --> 00:11:24.307
Can we tractor the vessel out?

00:11:24.348 --> 00:11:26.908
No. The subspace interference
is too heavy.

00:11:26.951 --> 00:11:30.352
Captain, we're less
than three light-years

00:11:30.388 --> 00:11:31.480
from llidaria.

00:11:31.522 --> 00:11:33.888
They have sophisticated
technology.

00:11:33.924 --> 00:11:34.982
They might be able to help

00:11:35.059 --> 00:11:37.289
and they're quite friendly...

00:11:37.328 --> 00:11:38.886
most of the time.

00:11:38.929 --> 00:11:40.897
No. It looks like
it's being pulled in

00:11:40.931 --> 00:11:42.262
toward the singularity.

00:11:42.300 --> 00:11:44.461
We have to get it out
of the event horizon.

00:11:44.502 --> 00:11:45.526
Bridge to Torres.

00:11:45.569 --> 00:11:47.161
We need a way to get
that ship out of there.

00:11:47.204 --> 00:11:48.228
Any ideas?

00:11:48.272 --> 00:11:50.433
I was thinking we could
remodulate a tractor beam

00:11:50.474 --> 00:11:52.567
to match the subspace
interference.

00:11:52.610 --> 00:11:54.771
It might be enough to cut
through the event horizon.

00:11:54.812 --> 00:11:56.279
A subspace tractor beam.

00:11:56.314 --> 00:11:57.281
Exactly.

00:11:57.315 --> 00:11:58.612
When can you have it ready?

00:11:58.649 --> 00:12:00.207
Two hours, maybe three.

00:12:00.251 --> 00:12:01.240
Get right on it.

00:12:01.285 --> 00:12:02.650
Use as many people as you need.

00:12:02.687 --> 00:12:05.520
Mr. Carey, what do you think?

00:12:06.524 --> 00:12:08.822
With the right field modulation,
it might work

00:12:08.859 --> 00:12:10.952
but we'll need more power
to the emitter array.

00:12:11.028 --> 00:12:12.757
Very well.
You're in charge, Mr. Carey.

00:12:12.797 --> 00:12:15.595
Report to me
when the tractor beam is ready.

00:12:15.633 --> 00:12:16.998
Aye, Captain.

00:12:21.639 --> 00:12:22.571
Mr. Paris.

00:12:22.606 --> 00:12:24.471
Hold our position here.

00:12:29.146 --> 00:12:31.239
I'd like to see you in private.

00:12:39.690 --> 00:12:40.657
We have a problem

00:12:40.691 --> 00:12:42.591
and I think it's time
we discuss it.

00:12:42.626 --> 00:12:44.491
Captain, I appreciate
your concerns

00:12:44.528 --> 00:12:45.961
about Torres,
but I promise you...

00:12:45.996 --> 00:12:47.156
You don't understand, Commander.

00:12:47.198 --> 00:12:49.132
This isn't about Torres.

00:12:49.166 --> 00:12:50.997
My problem is with you.

00:12:51.102 --> 00:12:52.797
Me?

00:12:52.837 --> 00:12:53.997
Let me be blunt.

00:12:54.038 --> 00:12:56.506
What you tried to do
just now was out of line.

00:12:56.540 --> 00:12:57.598
In what way?

00:12:57.641 --> 00:12:58.699
When you decided

00:12:58.743 --> 00:13:00.472
to call Torres in Engineering.

00:13:00.511 --> 00:13:01.478
I've worked with her.

00:13:01.512 --> 00:13:02.945
I know what she's capable of.

00:13:02.980 --> 00:13:04.447
We needed an answer right away

00:13:04.482 --> 00:13:06.074
and I knew
she could give us one.

00:13:06.117 --> 00:13:08.278
Carey is the Senior Officer
in Engineering.

00:13:08.319 --> 00:13:09.684
If you look at it that way,

00:13:09.720 --> 00:13:11.881
none of my people
will ever have seniority.

00:13:11.922 --> 00:13:13.947
That's the problem,
right there.

00:13:13.991 --> 00:13:15.390
They're not your people.

00:13:15.426 --> 00:13:16.916
You're treating
the Maquis on this ship

00:13:16.961 --> 00:13:18.428
like they're still your crew.

00:13:18.462 --> 00:13:20.930
I'm doing everything I can
to integrate them into your crew

00:13:20.965 --> 00:13:22.830
but frankly, you're not
making it easy for me, Captain.

00:13:22.867 --> 00:13:24.596
I can't make it easy, Commander.

00:13:24.635 --> 00:13:26.159
Surely you can understand that.

00:13:26.203 --> 00:13:27.295
They don't have the discipline.

00:13:27.338 --> 00:13:28.305
They don't have the training.

00:13:28.339 --> 00:13:29.772
But some of them,
like B'Elanna Torres

00:13:29.807 --> 00:13:30.899
have the ability.

00:13:30.941 --> 00:13:32.499
The Starfleet officers
on this ship

00:13:32.543 --> 00:13:34.977
have worked all their lives
to earn their commissions.

00:13:35.045 --> 00:13:36.512
How am I supposed to ask them

00:13:36.547 --> 00:13:38.777
to accept a Maquis
as their superior officer

00:13:38.816 --> 00:13:41.148
just because circumstances
have forced us together?

00:13:41.185 --> 00:13:43.153
You're asking them to accept me.

00:13:43.187 --> 00:13:44.279
You're qualified.

00:13:44.321 --> 00:13:45.618
You're a graduate of the Academy

00:13:45.656 --> 00:13:47.954
and you have
Starfleet command experience.

00:13:49.794 --> 00:13:51.352
Permission to speak freely.

00:13:51.395 --> 00:13:53.590
Go ahead.

00:13:53.631 --> 00:13:56.657
I have no intention of being
your token Maquis officer.

00:13:58.202 --> 00:13:59.999
Show me another qualified
Maquis candidate

00:14:00.037 --> 00:14:01.004
and I'll consider him.

00:14:01.038 --> 00:14:02.164
B'Elanna Torres.

00:14:02.206 --> 00:14:03.537
Who cannot control herself

00:14:03.574 --> 00:14:06.042
and who could not make it
through the Academy.

00:14:06.076 --> 00:14:07.407
She's the best engineer
I've ever known.

00:14:07.445 --> 00:14:09.174
She could teach at the Academy.

00:14:13.184 --> 00:14:14.879
You're right, Captain.

00:14:14.919 --> 00:14:16.944
I do consider these my people

00:14:17.054 --> 00:14:18.180
because nobody else on this ship

00:14:18.222 --> 00:14:19.780
will look out for them
like I will.

00:14:19.824 --> 00:14:20.882
And I'm telling you,

00:14:20.925 --> 00:14:22.517
you're going to have
to give them more authority

00:14:22.560 --> 00:14:23.720
if you want their loyalty.

00:14:23.761 --> 00:14:25.194
Theirs...

00:14:25.229 --> 00:14:26.924
or yours, Commander?

00:14:28.132 --> 00:14:29.895
I'm trying to help you.

00:14:29.934 --> 00:14:32.664
I'm sorry you don't see that.

00:14:34.538 --> 00:14:36.733
I strongly recommend
you get to know Torres

00:14:36.774 --> 00:14:38.833
before you choose
a new Chief Engineer.

00:14:40.377 --> 00:14:41.779
Permission to leave.

00:14:43.080 --> 00:14:44.638
Dismissed.

00:15:00.364 --> 00:15:03.629
Computer, activate the emergency
medical holographic system.

00:15:03.667 --> 00:15:06.227
Please state the nature
of the medical emergency.

00:15:06.270 --> 00:15:08.568
Actually, there is no emergency.

00:15:08.605 --> 00:15:10.232
I'm creating a hydroponics bay.

00:15:10.274 --> 00:15:11.866
I was told you could provide me

00:15:11.909 --> 00:15:13.740
with some nitrogenated
soil samples.

00:15:13.777 --> 00:15:15.142
That's it?

00:15:15.179 --> 00:15:15.941
I'm sorry if...

00:15:15.979 --> 00:15:17.810
So it begins.

00:15:17.848 --> 00:15:21.477
The trivia of medicine
is my domain now.

00:15:21.518 --> 00:15:25.215
Every runny nose,
stubbed toe, pimple on a cheek

00:15:25.255 --> 00:15:27.223
becomes my responsibility.

00:15:27.257 --> 00:15:28.781
You are the only doctor we have.

00:15:28.826 --> 00:15:31.226
I am not just a doctor.

00:15:31.261 --> 00:15:33.855
I've been designed
with the information

00:15:33.897 --> 00:15:35.831
from 2,000
medical reference sources

00:15:35.866 --> 00:15:38.960
and the experience of 47
individual medical officers.

00:15:39.069 --> 00:15:42.266
I am the embodiment
of modern medicine.

00:15:44.174 --> 00:15:46.369
How much dirt do you need?

00:15:46.410 --> 00:15:48.071
Four samples will be enough.

00:15:50.347 --> 00:15:52.338
Now I know how Hippocrates felt

00:15:52.382 --> 00:15:55.249
when the king needed
him to trim a hangnail.

00:15:55.285 --> 00:15:58.345
You're very sensitive,
aren't you?

00:16:01.124 --> 00:16:03.490
As a medical practitioner,

00:16:03.527 --> 00:16:07.861
I require a certain sensitivity
to properly address a patient.

00:16:09.499 --> 00:16:12.434
I'm talking about you
as a person.

00:16:13.904 --> 00:16:16.099
I am merely a hologram.

00:16:17.107 --> 00:16:19.575
Doctor, has your program
altered your appearance

00:16:19.610 --> 00:16:20.907
since I came to Sick Bay?

00:16:20.944 --> 00:16:22.468
No.

00:16:22.512 --> 00:16:24.002
Why?

00:16:25.482 --> 00:16:26.574
When I first came in

00:16:26.617 --> 00:16:28.915
your head was at the same
height as this cabinet

00:16:28.952 --> 00:16:33.150
but now you look at least
ten centimeters shorter.

00:16:43.367 --> 00:16:46.268
I've just run a diagnostic
on my imaging processor.

00:16:46.303 --> 00:16:49.830
It shows that I've been reduced
in height by 10.4 centimeters.

00:16:52.576 --> 00:16:54.476
Sick Bay to Operations.

00:16:54.511 --> 00:16:55.944
This is Kim.

00:16:55.979 --> 00:16:58.447
The holographic projector
in here is malfunctioning.

00:16:58.482 --> 00:16:59.972
Would you send a repair crew
down right away?

00:17:00.083 --> 00:17:01.277
We're a little busy right now.

00:17:01.318 --> 00:17:03.286
We'll get to it
as soon as we can.

00:17:03.320 --> 00:17:04.287
It's just that I'm...

00:17:04.321 --> 00:17:05.345
Kim out.

00:17:08.992 --> 00:17:11.085
Well...

00:17:11.128 --> 00:17:14.564
seems like a very busy day
in Operations.

00:17:14.598 --> 00:17:16.498
I'm sorry
I bothered you with this.

00:17:16.533 --> 00:17:17.591
No trouble at all.

00:17:17.634 --> 00:17:19.568
Just turn off the program
before you leave.

00:17:19.603 --> 00:17:20.968
What's your name?

00:17:22.039 --> 00:17:24.735
What purpose would a name
serve a hologram?

00:17:24.775 --> 00:17:27.107
I'd just like to know what
to call you besides "Doctor."

00:17:28.979 --> 00:17:30.207
I guess they never thought

00:17:30.247 --> 00:17:32.078
I'd be around
long enough to need one.

00:17:32.115 --> 00:17:33.776
What's your name?

00:17:33.817 --> 00:17:36.786
- Kes.
- Kes.

00:17:36.820 --> 00:17:40.051
I'm glad I could help you today.

00:17:40.090 --> 00:17:41.853
Computer, end program.

00:17:48.932 --> 00:17:50.263
Carey to Bridge.

00:17:50.300 --> 00:17:51.392
The subspace tractor beam

00:17:51.435 --> 00:17:53.198
is on line
and ready to go, Captain.

00:17:53.236 --> 00:17:54.225
Acknowledged.

00:17:54.271 --> 00:17:56.239
Mr. Tuvok, lock onto that ship.

00:17:56.273 --> 00:17:57.604
Engaging tractor beam.

00:18:01.979 --> 00:18:02.911
It's working.

00:18:02.980 --> 00:18:05.574
The beam is penetrating
the event horizon.

00:18:05.615 --> 00:18:07.082
Engineering,
check your power levels.

00:18:07.117 --> 00:18:09.176
I'm showing
massive fluctuations.

00:18:09.219 --> 00:18:11.153
Damn it! The new relays on
the power grid aren't holding!

00:18:12.122 --> 00:18:14.181
We're being pulled
toward the singularity!

00:18:14.224 --> 00:18:15.350
What's going on?

00:18:15.392 --> 00:18:17.656
Power to the tractor beam
is down 80 percent.

00:18:17.694 --> 00:18:19.252
The gravimetric force
of the singularity

00:18:19.296 --> 00:18:20.263
is pulling us in.

00:18:20.297 --> 00:18:22.060
Impulse engines, full reverse.

00:18:22.099 --> 00:18:23.566
Disengage the tractor beam.

00:18:23.600 --> 00:18:25.090
I can't shut it down!

00:18:25.135 --> 00:18:26.762
The emitter relays are locked.

00:18:26.803 --> 00:18:28.361
I'm picking up hull stress
all over the ship.

00:18:28.405 --> 00:18:29.736
If we keep engines
at full reverse

00:18:29.773 --> 00:18:31.070
while the tractor beam's
engaged,

00:18:31.108 --> 00:18:32.132
it'll pull the ship apart.

00:18:32.175 --> 00:18:33.301
Cut the engines!

00:18:33.343 --> 00:18:35.072
We're moving forward again.

00:18:35.112 --> 00:18:37.307
Engineering, get that
tractor beam off-line!

00:18:37.347 --> 00:18:39.042
Captain, I can shut it down,

00:18:39.082 --> 00:18:40.106
but I'll have to get in there

00:18:40.150 --> 00:18:41.708
and physically cut
the main power feed.

00:18:41.752 --> 00:18:43.617
Do it!

00:18:50.660 --> 00:18:52.753
Tractor beam disengaged.

00:18:52.796 --> 00:18:55.959
Move us to a safe distance,
Mr. Paris.

00:18:57.200 --> 00:18:59.361
Are we abandoning
the rescue attempt?

00:18:59.403 --> 00:19:01.871
No, but we are going
to need some help.

00:19:01.905 --> 00:19:04.169
Lay in a course
for the llidaria system.

00:19:04.207 --> 00:19:05.538
Full impulse.

00:19:05.575 --> 00:19:07.736
Have Mr. Neelix
report to the Bridge.

00:19:07.778 --> 00:19:08.745
It looks like we're

00:19:08.779 --> 00:19:10.610
going to follow his
suggestion after all.

00:19:35.972 --> 00:19:38.270
Come in.

00:19:38.308 --> 00:19:40.742
You asked to see me, Captain.

00:19:40.777 --> 00:19:42.642
Have a seat.

00:19:42.679 --> 00:19:44.044
I just want you to know

00:19:44.081 --> 00:19:47.608
that I have personally gone over
every emitter relay again

00:19:47.651 --> 00:19:49.551
and refitted
four of them myself.

00:19:49.586 --> 00:19:51.349
No one blames you
for what happened.

00:19:51.388 --> 00:19:54.050
Commander Chakotay
thinks very highly of you.

00:19:54.091 --> 00:19:57.083
He's recommended you
for Chief Engineer.

00:19:58.328 --> 00:20:00.728
Well, uh...

00:20:00.764 --> 00:20:03.528
we've, uh, we've been
through a few scrapes together.

00:20:05.535 --> 00:20:07.127
Do you think you're ready?

00:20:07.170 --> 00:20:08.637
Ready?

00:20:08.672 --> 00:20:12.005
Ready to be Chief Engineer
on a starship.

00:20:13.143 --> 00:20:15.668
Well, I think I know my way
around an Engine Room,

00:20:15.712 --> 00:20:16.872
if that's what you mean.

00:20:16.913 --> 00:20:19.677
It's part of what I mean.

00:20:21.251 --> 00:20:22.741
There's also the matter

00:20:22.786 --> 00:20:24.981
of your ability
to command others.

00:20:25.055 --> 00:20:27.353
I'm not sure
I'd be doing you a favor

00:20:27.390 --> 00:20:29.688
by putting you in charge
down there.

00:20:29.726 --> 00:20:31.887
There'd be a lot
of hard feelings toward you

00:20:31.928 --> 00:20:33.623
from many
of the Starfleet people.

00:20:33.663 --> 00:20:37.622
I'm not bothered
by what people think of me.

00:20:37.667 --> 00:20:40.568
And the job requires knowledge
of Starfleet protocol,

00:20:40.604 --> 00:20:43.004
experience with Starfleet
methodologies...

00:20:43.039 --> 00:20:44.097
Listen, Captain

00:20:44.141 --> 00:20:46.268
if you don't think
I'm right for this job

00:20:46.309 --> 00:20:47.708
just say so.

00:20:49.212 --> 00:20:51.407
I'll be honest.

00:20:51.448 --> 00:20:54.645
I'm not sure
whether you are or not.

00:20:54.684 --> 00:20:55.946
That's why I wanted to meet,

00:20:55.986 --> 00:20:57.749
try to get to know
you a little better.

00:21:00.924 --> 00:21:03.620
I've been studying
your Academy record.

00:21:05.362 --> 00:21:07.057
Where did you get that?

00:21:07.097 --> 00:21:09.190
Thanks to Tuvok,
we had the names

00:21:09.232 --> 00:21:12.497
of your entire crew
by the time we left DS9.

00:21:13.637 --> 00:21:16.800
Four disciplinary hearings,
one suspension.

00:21:16.840 --> 00:21:18.865
You had quite a turbulent
couple of years,

00:21:18.909 --> 00:21:20.103
didn't you?

00:21:20.143 --> 00:21:21.838
Yeah, I guess
you could say that.

00:21:21.878 --> 00:21:23.345
What do you think
the problem was?

00:21:23.380 --> 00:21:26.076
The problem?
The problem was a system

00:21:26.116 --> 00:21:27.708
that didn't give anyone
a chance to breathe.

00:21:27.751 --> 00:21:29.981
We work under that same system
on this ship.

00:21:30.020 --> 00:21:33.080
Then I guess maybe
this is just a bad idea.

00:21:33.123 --> 00:21:34.784
Why did you quit the Academy,
B'Elanna?

00:21:37.861 --> 00:21:40.625
I didn't want to have anything
to do with Starfleet then,

00:21:40.664 --> 00:21:42.689
and I'm sorry
that I have to now.

00:21:46.836 --> 00:21:48.827
Sick Bay to Captain Janeway.

00:21:48.872 --> 00:21:50.169
Yes, Doctor?

00:21:50.207 --> 00:21:53.836
May I request you activate
monitor input 47...

00:21:53.877 --> 00:21:56.243
your emergency
medical holograph channel?

00:21:56.279 --> 00:21:57.268
Of course.

00:22:02.785 --> 00:22:05.083
I'm getting
a distorted transmission.

00:22:06.155 --> 00:22:08.919
No. In fact, that is
how I appear at present.

00:22:08.958 --> 00:22:11.518
I'm... shrinking.

00:22:11.560 --> 00:22:12.549
Shrinking?

00:22:12.595 --> 00:22:14.062
There appears
to be a malfunction

00:22:14.096 --> 00:22:15.085
in my imaging system.

00:22:15.131 --> 00:22:16.359
It's been reducing my height

00:22:16.399 --> 00:22:18.196
by five centimeters every hour.

00:22:18.234 --> 00:22:20.429
I've been waiting all day
for repairs.

00:22:20.469 --> 00:22:23.438
That is not, however,
what I am calling about.

00:22:23.472 --> 00:22:25.940
Over the past three hours,
nine crew members have reported

00:22:25.975 --> 00:22:27.943
unusual symptoms...
severe headaches,

00:22:27.977 --> 00:22:30.707
muscle spasms
and sudden waves of dizziness.

00:22:30.746 --> 00:22:34.079
All of this could be related
to the quantum singularity.

00:22:34.116 --> 00:22:35.674
Quantum singularity?

00:22:35.718 --> 00:22:39.176
It was sending out
strong spatial distortions.

00:22:39.221 --> 00:22:41.781
They might have affected
the crew

00:22:41.824 --> 00:22:43.382
and your systems as well.

00:22:43.426 --> 00:22:45.223
You know, Captain,
if we were in the vicinity

00:22:45.261 --> 00:22:48.662
of a quantum singularity,
I should have been informed.

00:22:48.697 --> 00:22:49.994
You're right.

00:22:50.032 --> 00:22:52.523
I'll look into linking your
program into the ship's data.

00:22:56.238 --> 00:22:58.229
Hello? Captain?

00:22:59.408 --> 00:23:00.500
Hello!

00:23:00.543 --> 00:23:01.771
Report.

00:23:01.811 --> 00:23:03.870
It looks like we're running
into more spatial distortions.

00:23:03.913 --> 00:23:04.937
I'm picking up a highly

00:23:04.980 --> 00:23:06.413
localized disturbance
off the port bow.

00:23:06.449 --> 00:23:07.746
On screen.

00:23:10.786 --> 00:23:11.946
Mr. Tuvok?

00:23:11.987 --> 00:23:15.150
It is another type-4
quantum singularity.

00:23:15.191 --> 00:23:17.785
Physical and temporal
dimensions are identical

00:23:17.827 --> 00:23:20.295
to the one
we encountered earlier.

00:23:20.329 --> 00:23:21.591
Captain, according
to these readings,

00:23:21.630 --> 00:23:23.393
we've returned
to our previous coordinates.

00:23:24.600 --> 00:23:26.591
This isn't another singularity.

00:23:27.603 --> 00:23:29.264
It's the same one.

00:23:42.518 --> 00:23:43.985
Check the navigational logs.

00:23:44.019 --> 00:23:45.145
Confirm our position.

00:23:46.222 --> 00:23:47.211
Well, they show we've traveled

00:23:47.256 --> 00:23:49.554
1.4 light-years away
from the anomaly,

00:23:49.592 --> 00:23:51.856
but I've confirmed our position
against the star chart.

00:23:51.894 --> 00:23:53.794
We're definitely back
where we started.

00:23:53.829 --> 00:23:54.853
It doesn't make sense.

00:23:54.897 --> 00:23:56.330
They can't both be right.

00:23:56.365 --> 00:23:58.890
We're either still at the
singularity or we're not.

00:23:58.934 --> 00:24:00.299
Well, we can see

00:24:00.336 --> 00:24:02.133
the singularity,
so I tend to believe

00:24:02.171 --> 00:24:04.162
that the external sensors
are correct.

00:24:04.206 --> 00:24:05.434
That would imply there was

00:24:05.474 --> 00:24:06.805
something wrong
with the warp drive

00:24:06.842 --> 00:24:07.934
and the navigational logs.

00:24:08.010 --> 00:24:11.309
Mr. Paris, lay in a course
away from the singularity.

00:24:11.347 --> 00:24:13.372
This time at maximum warp.

00:24:13.415 --> 00:24:14.939
Ensign Kim, I want you

00:24:14.984 --> 00:24:16.474
to keep a sensor lock
on the singularity.

00:24:16.519 --> 00:24:18.544
Verify that we're moving
away from it.

00:24:20.022 --> 00:24:21.080
Engage.

00:24:27.129 --> 00:24:28.391
Distance from the singularity

00:24:28.430 --> 00:24:30.261
is ten million kilometers
and increasing.

00:24:30.299 --> 00:24:31.926
Warp engines holding steady.

00:24:31.967 --> 00:24:34.162
All systems report normal.

00:24:34.203 --> 00:24:35.363
11 million.

00:24:36.539 --> 00:24:37.563
12 million.

00:24:41.443 --> 00:24:42.410
I don't get it.

00:24:42.444 --> 00:24:44.105
Mr. Paris,
have we altered course?

00:24:44.146 --> 00:24:48.913
No, we're still steady
on 310 mark 215.

00:24:48.951 --> 00:24:50.646
Then something's
really wrong here.

00:24:50.686 --> 00:24:52.483
It's ahead of us again.

00:24:52.521 --> 00:24:53.852
Confirmed.

00:24:53.889 --> 00:24:55.550
We're back
at the same coordinates.

00:24:55.591 --> 00:24:57.286
All stop.

00:25:01.830 --> 00:25:03.798
Ensign Kim,
transfer all the data

00:25:03.832 --> 00:25:05.424
on the spatial distortions
we encountered

00:25:05.467 --> 00:25:06.434
to my ready room.

00:25:06.468 --> 00:25:08.163
I'm going to begin
my own analysis.

00:25:08.203 --> 00:25:11.934
Commander, have all departments
run complete system diagnostics.

00:25:12.041 --> 00:25:13.941
I'll expect a full report
from all senior officers

00:25:14.043 --> 00:25:15.840
at 1500 hours.

00:25:17.212 --> 00:25:18.941
Who will represent Engineering?

00:25:19.048 --> 00:25:20.948
Lieutenant Carey.

00:25:21.050 --> 00:25:23.382
You should invite
B'Elanna Torres as well,

00:25:23.419 --> 00:25:25.683
unless you've removed her
from consideration.

00:25:25.721 --> 00:25:27.552
Fine.

00:25:42.938 --> 00:25:44.235
The Captain says she wants you

00:25:44.273 --> 00:25:46.241
to attend the staff
meeting this afternoon.

00:25:46.275 --> 00:25:48.072
Oh.

00:25:48.110 --> 00:25:51.136
Well, I'll give you
a full report on what happens.

00:25:51.180 --> 00:25:53.410
I'll be there, too.

00:25:53.449 --> 00:25:56.179
Just remember, Torres,
I'm the Senior Officer

00:25:56.218 --> 00:25:57.947
and I speak for Engineering.

00:25:57.987 --> 00:25:58.976
Try not to say anything

00:25:59.088 --> 00:26:00.783
unless someone
asks you a question.

00:26:03.192 --> 00:26:05.251
1500 hours, Deck 1.

00:26:05.294 --> 00:26:06.989
Don't be late.

00:26:11.934 --> 00:26:14.926
You should have broken
more than his nose.

00:26:21.010 --> 00:26:23.069
I've taken a look
at every major subsystem

00:26:23.112 --> 00:26:24.670
and there's no sign
of any trouble.

00:26:24.713 --> 00:26:26.180
What about
the navigational array?

00:26:26.215 --> 00:26:27.944
We'll have the results
in about an hour.

00:26:27.983 --> 00:26:30.417
So, I hear there's been
some trouble

00:26:30.452 --> 00:26:31.851
down in Engineering.

00:26:31.887 --> 00:26:34.287
There was an altercation,
but it has been resolved.

00:26:34.323 --> 00:26:35.551
Oh, that's not what I hear.

00:26:35.591 --> 00:26:37.582
I hear that the whole
department's up in arms.

00:26:37.626 --> 00:26:38.820
That the Maquis
and Starfleet people

00:26:38.861 --> 00:26:40.055
are almost
at each other's throats.

00:26:40.095 --> 00:26:41.926
I will never cease to be amazed

00:26:41.964 --> 00:26:44.159
by the human capacity
for hyperbole.

00:26:44.199 --> 00:26:47.635
The situation may be
characterized as tense,

00:26:47.670 --> 00:26:48.796
but one could hardly say

00:26:48.837 --> 00:26:50.668
that they are about
to become violent.

00:26:51.807 --> 00:26:52.967
Well, I just hope you're right.

00:26:53.042 --> 00:26:55.533
I mean, things are bad enough
around here without... ow!

00:26:55.577 --> 00:26:56.771
Are you all right?

00:26:56.812 --> 00:26:59.406
Yeah. I just have this
splitting headache, and... ow!

00:27:01.383 --> 00:27:02.577
I'd better get you to Sick Bay.

00:27:02.618 --> 00:27:03.710
Just dizzy. Can't...

00:27:07.122 --> 00:27:08.282
Can't focus.

00:27:09.625 --> 00:27:12.822
27 other crew members
have now reported the symptoms

00:27:12.861 --> 00:27:14.385
and yet, when I
examined each of them,

00:27:14.430 --> 00:27:15.897
I couldn't find anything wrong.

00:27:15.931 --> 00:27:18.399
I have no diagnosis,
no prognosis,

00:27:18.434 --> 00:27:19.662
no recommended treatment.

00:27:19.702 --> 00:27:21.795
I'll contact you when I have
something more to contribute.

00:27:21.837 --> 00:27:26.206
And by the way, I am now
68 centimeters shorter.

00:27:26.241 --> 00:27:27.606
I'd appreciate it if someone

00:27:27.643 --> 00:27:29.508
would repair
my holographic projectors

00:27:29.545 --> 00:27:32.309
before I have trouble
reaching my patients.

00:27:35.217 --> 00:27:37.515
I've finished my study
of the spatial distortions

00:27:37.553 --> 00:27:39.214
coming from the singularity.

00:27:39.254 --> 00:27:41.518
I can give you
a long, boring analysis.

00:27:41.557 --> 00:27:43.957
Suffice it to say, I don't
know what's going on.

00:27:44.059 --> 00:27:46.584
The readings are confused,
the data is contradictory.

00:27:46.628 --> 00:27:48.721
None of it makes any sense.

00:27:48.764 --> 00:27:51.096
Anyone have anything
more constructive?

00:27:51.133 --> 00:27:52.623
Ship-wide diagnostics

00:27:52.668 --> 00:27:54.363
have revealed nothing
out of the ordinary.

00:27:54.403 --> 00:27:56.132
I tried sending out
a tachyon signal,

00:27:56.171 --> 00:27:59.197
to scan the singularity,
but all I got back was static.

00:27:59.241 --> 00:28:01.300
B'Elanna, do you think
you can work with Mr. Carey

00:28:01.343 --> 00:28:02.367
to clean up that signal?

00:28:04.713 --> 00:28:05.737
Yes.

00:28:05.781 --> 00:28:07.078
Good.

00:28:07.116 --> 00:28:08.413
But it won't work.

00:28:09.551 --> 00:28:11.178
I mean, it was
a good idea to try it,

00:28:11.220 --> 00:28:13.347
but... it won't work.

00:28:13.388 --> 00:28:15.913
You have another idea?

00:28:17.559 --> 00:28:19.754
I was just thinking
about the problem

00:28:19.795 --> 00:28:22.229
with the Doctor's
holographic projectors.

00:28:22.264 --> 00:28:24.494
It seems like
the spatial distortions

00:28:24.533 --> 00:28:25.500
might be interfering

00:28:25.534 --> 00:28:27.297
with the projector's
phase alignment.

00:28:27.336 --> 00:28:29.270
That was my guess, too.

00:28:29.304 --> 00:28:30.271
If that's the case,

00:28:30.305 --> 00:28:31.533
I could screen out
those distortions

00:28:31.573 --> 00:28:32.938
by setting up a
localized damping field

00:28:33.008 --> 00:28:33.940
around the projectors.

00:28:34.009 --> 00:28:35.374
Is our priority here really

00:28:35.410 --> 00:28:36.934
the medical holograph system?

00:28:36.979 --> 00:28:40.574
My point is that if
the spatial distortions

00:28:40.616 --> 00:28:42.481
are also interfering
with the transmission

00:28:42.518 --> 00:28:44.509
we received
from the other ship...

00:28:44.553 --> 00:28:47.386
We could set up a similar field
around our external sensors

00:28:47.422 --> 00:28:48.889
and communicate with them.

00:28:48.924 --> 00:28:50.118
Exactly.

00:28:50.159 --> 00:28:51.956
And they may know more about
what's going on than we do.

00:28:52.060 --> 00:28:53.357
Let's give it a try.

00:28:53.395 --> 00:28:54.760
Dismissed.

00:29:15.616 --> 00:29:17.607
Emitters on line.

00:29:17.652 --> 00:29:19.586
I'm rerouting the damping field

00:29:19.620 --> 00:29:21.520
through the deflector grid.

00:29:21.555 --> 00:29:23.420
Open a channel
to the other ship.

00:29:23.457 --> 00:29:27.257
This is... singularity...

00:29:27.295 --> 00:29:28.819
Ramping up field intensity.

00:29:31.932 --> 00:29:33.229
It's working.

00:29:33.267 --> 00:29:35.064
We're cutting through
the distortions.

00:29:35.102 --> 00:29:39.539
This is... to the vessel
near the...

00:29:39.573 --> 00:29:41.473
Remodulate the EM band.

00:29:41.509 --> 00:29:43.306
See if you can clear it up
a little more.

00:29:43.344 --> 00:29:46.313
Compensating
for amplitude distortion.

00:29:48.149 --> 00:29:50.845
This is Captain Kathryn Janeway
of the Starship Voyager

00:29:50.885 --> 00:29:53.513
to the vessel
near the quantum singularity.

00:29:53.554 --> 00:29:55.044
Do you need help?

00:29:56.524 --> 00:29:57.616
That's your hail.

00:29:57.658 --> 00:30:00.957
I'm applying the damping field
to our visual scanners.

00:30:00.995 --> 00:30:02.462
I'll see if I can clarify
the image.

00:30:07.735 --> 00:30:09.134
It's the Voyager.

00:30:09.170 --> 00:30:10.637
It's us.

00:30:24.018 --> 00:30:26.953
Sensors confirm
it's definitely USS Voyager.

00:30:26.987 --> 00:30:28.978
I've been hailing the ship
on all frequencies.

00:30:29.090 --> 00:30:30.717
No response.

00:30:30.758 --> 00:30:32.623
I don't think you'll get
a response, Lieutenant.

00:30:32.660 --> 00:30:33.888
That message we unscrambled

00:30:33.928 --> 00:30:36.988
is the same message I sent out
nine hours ago.

00:30:37.098 --> 00:30:38.690
Could we have traveled
back in time

00:30:38.733 --> 00:30:39.757
and met ourselves?

00:30:39.800 --> 00:30:40.858
No, that's not it.

00:30:42.536 --> 00:30:45.027
I think I have an explanation.

00:30:49.777 --> 00:30:51.608
Think of it like this.

00:30:51.645 --> 00:30:53.806
You're sitting
at the bottom of a pond,

00:30:53.848 --> 00:30:55.748
which is frozen over

00:30:55.783 --> 00:30:58.115
and you look up
at the surface and you see

00:30:58.152 --> 00:30:59.847
a reflection of yourself.

00:30:59.887 --> 00:31:02.321
Now you might think you're
looking at another person

00:31:02.356 --> 00:31:04.517
sitting at the bottom
of another pond

00:31:04.558 --> 00:31:05.525
looking back at you.

00:31:05.559 --> 00:31:07.390
And in this case,
we'd be staring up

00:31:07.428 --> 00:31:09.055
at the surface
of the event horizon

00:31:09.096 --> 00:31:11.564
and seeing a time-delayed
reflection of ourselves.

00:31:11.599 --> 00:31:12.566
Are you saying...

00:31:12.600 --> 00:31:14.397
we're the ones trapped
in the singularity?

00:31:14.435 --> 00:31:15.561
Unfortunately, yes.

00:31:15.603 --> 00:31:16.570
You're right.

00:31:16.604 --> 00:31:17.935
It's the only theory
that explains

00:31:17.972 --> 00:31:19.303
everything
that's happened to us.

00:31:19.340 --> 00:31:20.830
We've probably been
in this singularity

00:31:20.875 --> 00:31:22.308
since we felt the first jolt.

00:31:22.343 --> 00:31:23.605
Wait a minute, wait.

00:31:23.644 --> 00:31:25.771
Wait a minute.
Let me get this straight.

00:31:25.813 --> 00:31:27.678
We were cruising along
at warp seven.

00:31:27.715 --> 00:31:29.182
Then we pick up a distress call

00:31:29.216 --> 00:31:30.513
and moved in to investigate.

00:31:30.551 --> 00:31:33.816
But now, you're saying
that the other ship

00:31:33.854 --> 00:31:34.980
is actually
just a reflection of us

00:31:35.055 --> 00:31:37.990
and that the distress call
is actually

00:31:38.025 --> 00:31:39.515
just the Captain's opening hail.

00:31:39.560 --> 00:31:42.791
But we picked up
the distress call

00:31:42.830 --> 00:31:44.559
before she sent the hail.

00:31:44.598 --> 00:31:46.623
How could we have been seeing

00:31:46.667 --> 00:31:48.532
a reflection of something
we hadn't even done yet?

00:31:51.906 --> 00:31:53.897
Am I making any sense here?

00:31:53.941 --> 00:31:55.875
No, but that's okay.

00:31:55.910 --> 00:31:57.844
One of the more difficult
concepts to grasp

00:31:57.878 --> 00:31:58.867
in temporal mechanics

00:31:58.913 --> 00:32:01.677
is that sometimes
effect can precede cause.

00:32:01.715 --> 00:32:05.082
A reaction can be observed
before the action

00:32:05.119 --> 00:32:06.450
which initiated it.

00:32:06.487 --> 00:32:08.114
So, what do we do to get out?

00:32:08.155 --> 00:32:09.349
I'm not sure,

00:32:09.390 --> 00:32:11.358
but I do know one thing.

00:32:11.392 --> 00:32:13.383
That as we slide deeper
into the singularity,

00:32:13.427 --> 00:32:15.691
the spacial distortions
are increasing.

00:32:15.729 --> 00:32:17.424
According to my calculations,

00:32:17.465 --> 00:32:20.161
within nine hours,
they'll crush the ship.

00:32:20.201 --> 00:32:21.293
If your analogy's

00:32:21.335 --> 00:32:23.963
correct, how do we get
through that sheet of ice?

00:32:24.004 --> 00:32:24.971
Look for a crack.

00:32:25.005 --> 00:32:25.972
Or make a crack.

00:32:26.006 --> 00:32:27.405
Take something
and smack it into the ice

00:32:27.441 --> 00:32:28.567
until it buckles.

00:32:28.609 --> 00:32:29.576
Wait a minute.

00:32:29.610 --> 00:32:31.134
What if we've already
made a crack in the ice?

00:32:31.178 --> 00:32:32.509
When we first entered
the event horizon.

00:32:32.546 --> 00:32:33.604
If we could find
our entry point,

00:32:33.647 --> 00:32:35.239
we might be able to slip out
the way we came in.

00:32:35.282 --> 00:32:37.580
So, we'd be looking
for a subspace instability

00:32:37.618 --> 00:32:38.676
in the event horizon.

00:32:38.719 --> 00:32:40.983
What would make it show
up on our sensors?

00:32:41.088 --> 00:32:42.282
Warp particles.

00:32:42.323 --> 00:32:44.587
If we saturate the event horizon
with warp particles,

00:32:44.625 --> 00:32:45.990
we might be able
to see them escaping

00:32:46.060 --> 00:32:47.789
through the rupture
we made when we entered.

00:32:49.296 --> 00:32:52.163
Mr. Tuvok, take the main
deflector off-line.

00:32:52.199 --> 00:32:54.463
Mr. Kim, reroute the port
and starboard plasma flow

00:32:54.502 --> 00:32:55.662
to the main deflector.

00:32:55.703 --> 00:32:57.330
We can use it
to generate a warp field.

00:32:57.371 --> 00:32:58.429
Deflectors off-line.

00:32:58.472 --> 00:33:00.906
Initializing plasma flow.

00:33:00.941 --> 00:33:03.034
Release the warp particles.

00:33:09.149 --> 00:33:10.741
Scanning the singularity.

00:33:10.784 --> 00:33:11.773
Anything?

00:33:11.819 --> 00:33:12.786
Not yet.

00:33:12.820 --> 00:33:14.253
Warp particles
at full intensity.

00:33:15.356 --> 00:33:18.792
I'm picking up something...
a slight irregularity.

00:33:18.826 --> 00:33:20.851
It could be a rupture
in the event horizon.

00:33:22.296 --> 00:33:24.093
Put it on screen.

00:33:26.800 --> 00:33:28.631
It is a rupture, Captain.

00:33:28.669 --> 00:33:31.365
It's 50 meters by...

00:33:31.405 --> 00:33:33.100
ten meters.

00:33:33.140 --> 00:33:34.334
Oh, it's too small.

00:33:34.375 --> 00:33:36.206
It must have collapsed since
we first passed through it.

00:33:36.243 --> 00:33:37.210
We found the crack.

00:33:37.244 --> 00:33:38.233
That's the important thing.

00:33:38.279 --> 00:33:39.507
Now, how do we make it bigger?

00:33:39.547 --> 00:33:41.845
Put a wedge in it
and force it open.

00:33:41.882 --> 00:33:43.509
We could try a dekyon beam.

00:33:43.551 --> 00:33:44.813
All right, a dekyon beam.

00:33:44.852 --> 00:33:46.843
Mr. Paris, bring us closer.

00:33:48.188 --> 00:33:49.746
Captain, if we get too close
to the rupture,

00:33:49.790 --> 00:33:50.757
our warp engines

00:33:50.791 --> 00:33:52.486
might make it collapse
even further.

00:33:52.526 --> 00:33:54.653
Can we emit a beam from here?

00:33:54.695 --> 00:33:56.287
The rupture's over 50 million
kilometers away.

00:33:56.330 --> 00:33:57.422
We don't have enough power.

00:33:57.464 --> 00:33:58.863
To project
a dekyon beam that far.

00:33:58.899 --> 00:34:00.662
We'll have to take
a shuttlecraft.

00:34:00.701 --> 00:34:02.498
You'll need the best pilot

00:34:02.536 --> 00:34:04.163
you've got in that shuttle,
Captain.

00:34:04.204 --> 00:34:05.831
That'll be me.

00:34:05.873 --> 00:34:07.773
Getting there is
the easy part, Mr. Paris.

00:34:07.808 --> 00:34:08.934
We need someone who's familiar

00:34:09.043 --> 00:34:11.204
with the finer points
of temporal mechanics,

00:34:11.245 --> 00:34:13.110
and unless you've been
hiding your credentials,

00:34:13.147 --> 00:34:14.944
I don't think that's you.

00:34:15.015 --> 00:34:16.642
B'Elanna, you're with me.

00:34:16.684 --> 00:34:18.151
The Bridge is yours, Commander.

00:34:37.371 --> 00:34:38.963
We've cleared Voyager.

00:34:39.039 --> 00:34:41.337
Shields at full strength.

00:34:41.375 --> 00:34:42.535
We'll reach the rupture

00:34:42.576 --> 00:34:44.669
in approximately
four and a half minutes.

00:34:44.712 --> 00:34:46.543
Let's get that dekyon beam
on line.

00:34:46.580 --> 00:34:48.070
Remodulating emitters.

00:34:54.989 --> 00:35:01.690
Captain... I want to apologize
for losing my temper.

00:35:01.729 --> 00:35:03.629
In your ready room?

00:35:03.664 --> 00:35:05.689
I think maybe you were hitting

00:35:05.733 --> 00:35:08.566
a little bit too close
to home, you know?

00:35:08.602 --> 00:35:11.571
I respect Chakotay,

00:35:11.605 --> 00:35:12.697
but he's wrong.

00:35:12.740 --> 00:35:15.607
I'm not officer material
and we both know it.

00:35:15.643 --> 00:35:19.602
The truth is, I quit
the Academy because...

00:35:19.647 --> 00:35:22.445
I realized I couldn't
make it in Starfleet.

00:35:22.483 --> 00:35:24.951
And, believe me, no one
was sorry to see me go.

00:35:27.755 --> 00:35:29.382
Professor Chapman was.

00:35:29.423 --> 00:35:30.822
What?

00:35:32.926 --> 00:35:35.417
He put a letter
in your permanent file

00:35:35.462 --> 00:35:38.954
saying that, should you ever
reapply, he would support you.

00:35:39.066 --> 00:35:40.693
He thought you were
one of the most

00:35:40.734 --> 00:35:42.395
promising cadets
he'd ever taught.

00:35:45.005 --> 00:35:47.803
I fought with him
almost every day.

00:35:47.841 --> 00:35:51.868
I was always questioning his,
his methods, his assumptions,

00:35:51.912 --> 00:35:54.881
and he was always slapping
me down like some upstart kid.

00:35:54.915 --> 00:35:57.975
I was surprised he didn't
help me pack my bags.

00:35:58.018 --> 00:35:59.383
Some professors like students

00:35:59.420 --> 00:36:01.684
who challenge their assumptions,
B'Elanna.

00:36:02.823 --> 00:36:04.916
And so do some captains.

00:36:04.958 --> 00:36:07.560
Professor Chapman wasn't alone.

00:36:07.693 --> 00:36:09.524
Many of your teachers
thought you had the potential

00:36:09.562 --> 00:36:11.689
to be an outstanding officer.

00:36:13.232 --> 00:36:15.894
You had more friends at the
Academy than you realized.

00:36:18.671 --> 00:36:20.935
We're 15 kilometers
from the rupture.

00:36:21.006 --> 00:36:23.440
Start charging the dekyon beam.

00:36:23.475 --> 00:36:24.464
Dekyon beam on line.

00:36:27.680 --> 00:36:29.944
Shields down to 62 percent.

00:36:30.015 --> 00:36:31.107
Increase speed.

00:36:31.150 --> 00:36:32.515
We have to get to that rupture

00:36:32.551 --> 00:36:34.712
before these spatial distortions
tear us apart.

00:36:43.095 --> 00:36:44.119
All right.

00:36:44.163 --> 00:36:47.428
Let's open this hole in the ice
a little wider.

00:36:47.466 --> 00:36:49.366
Initiate the dekyon beam.

00:36:51.003 --> 00:36:53.801
They've widened the rupture
by almost 35 percent.

00:36:53.839 --> 00:36:55.568
They'll have to widen it
twice that much

00:36:55.608 --> 00:36:57.200
before Voyager
can make it through.

00:36:57.242 --> 00:36:59.301
Hold our position.

00:36:59.345 --> 00:37:01.905
Mr. Tuvok, report.

00:37:01.981 --> 00:37:04.415
The spatial distortions
are increasing.

00:37:04.450 --> 00:37:06.645
Hull integrity has dropped
18 percent.

00:37:06.685 --> 00:37:09.051
We've widened the opening
by over 65 percent.

00:37:09.088 --> 00:37:10.555
I'd like another five
for safety.

00:37:10.589 --> 00:37:11.715
We're losing power.

00:37:11.757 --> 00:37:13.520
I don't think we're going
to get any more, Captain.

00:37:17.463 --> 00:37:20.091
Let's get back to Voyager.

00:37:20.132 --> 00:37:22.532
The shuttle is returning,
Commander.

00:37:22.568 --> 00:37:24.695
It appears as though
they've been damaged.

00:37:24.737 --> 00:37:26.534
Their com system's down.

00:37:26.572 --> 00:37:28.039
I am unable to raise them.

00:37:28.073 --> 00:37:30.769
The rupture's now 120 meters
in diameter.

00:37:30.809 --> 00:37:31.867
That leaves us with less

00:37:31.910 --> 00:37:33.878
than two meters' clearance
on either side.

00:37:38.450 --> 00:37:42.580
Mr. Paris, as soon as we've
recovered the shuttlecraft,

00:37:42.621 --> 00:37:44.418
lay in a course
toward the rupture

00:37:44.456 --> 00:37:46.287
and take us out.

00:37:46.325 --> 00:37:47.383
Aye, sir.

00:37:55.934 --> 00:37:57.595
Well, this is a problem.

00:37:57.636 --> 00:38:00.434
One of them is
a temporal reflection

00:38:00.472 --> 00:38:01.996
but which one?

00:38:03.776 --> 00:38:05.471
I'm getting identical readings
from both ships.

00:38:05.511 --> 00:38:07.809
Captain, the rupture
is starting to collapse again.

00:38:07.846 --> 00:38:09.279
If Voyager doesn't
get through it

00:38:09.314 --> 00:38:11.339
within the next five minutes,
they'll never get out.

00:38:11.383 --> 00:38:12.350
That means we have

00:38:12.384 --> 00:38:14.409
one chance
to pick the correct ship.

00:38:14.453 --> 00:38:16.080
Simple choice...

00:38:16.121 --> 00:38:19.215
port or starboard?

00:38:19.258 --> 00:38:21.123
- Starboard.
- Port.

00:38:22.594 --> 00:38:25.461
The port ship is more likely
to be the real one.

00:38:25.497 --> 00:38:26.623
It's closer to the rupture.

00:38:26.665 --> 00:38:30.066
That means... that means
they're holding position

00:38:30.102 --> 00:38:31.831
as close as they can
to the rupture,

00:38:31.870 --> 00:38:34.338
waiting for us to dock
before they head out.

00:38:34.373 --> 00:38:35.635
No. It may be closer
to the rupture,

00:38:35.674 --> 00:38:36.936
but it's facing
the wrong direction.

00:38:37.009 --> 00:38:38.943
The starboard ship is
facing away from us.

00:38:38.977 --> 00:38:40.205
That means

00:38:40.245 --> 00:38:42.145
they're trying to give us
easy access to the shuttlebay.

00:38:42.181 --> 00:38:44.274
There has to be some way
to tell them apart

00:38:44.316 --> 00:38:45.647
besides which direction
they're facing.

00:38:45.684 --> 00:38:48.380
The starboard ship's thrusters
are at standby.

00:38:48.420 --> 00:38:49.785
They're holding position

00:38:49.822 --> 00:38:52.882
But the port ship is
moving toward the rupture.

00:38:52.925 --> 00:38:54.449
Then I was right.
It's the port ship.

00:38:54.493 --> 00:38:55.460
They're heading out.

00:38:55.494 --> 00:38:56.552
Don't you see?

00:38:56.595 --> 00:38:58.187
Voyager did move
toward the rupture

00:38:58.230 --> 00:39:00.164
20 minutes ago
when we first discovered it.

00:39:00.199 --> 00:39:03.134
That's why the port ship
is moving toward it now.

00:39:03.168 --> 00:39:06.035
It's a time-delayed reflection
of what we did before.

00:39:06.071 --> 00:39:08.005
The starboard ship's
the real one.

00:39:08.040 --> 00:39:09.837
They're waiting for us.

00:39:11.009 --> 00:39:12.271
If you're wrong...

00:39:14.346 --> 00:39:16.974
we're going to have
a long time to debate it.

00:39:35.534 --> 00:39:36.933
We're ready to dock.

00:39:37.002 --> 00:39:38.333
All right.

00:39:38.370 --> 00:39:39.394
Cut the thrusters.

00:39:39.438 --> 00:39:42.032
Let's see what happens.

00:39:50.949 --> 00:39:53.611
Feels like a real ship to me.

00:39:53.652 --> 00:39:55.142
Let's get back to the Bridge.

00:39:59.925 --> 00:40:01.654
Commander Chakotay, report.

00:40:01.693 --> 00:40:02.853
We're almost to the rupture.

00:40:02.895 --> 00:40:04.954
Mr. Paris is about to impress us
with his piloting skills.

00:40:04.997 --> 00:40:05.964
The rupture's collapsing.

00:40:06.031 --> 00:40:07.089
It's down to 110 meters wide.

00:40:07.132 --> 00:40:08.121
That's too narrow.

00:40:08.167 --> 00:40:09.759
We're not going to make it.

00:40:14.540 --> 00:40:17.873
In command school, they taught
us to always remember

00:40:17.910 --> 00:40:21.903
that maneuvering a starship
is a very delicate process,

00:40:21.947 --> 00:40:25.974
but over the years,
I've learned that sometimes

00:40:26.018 --> 00:40:29.385
you just have to punch
your way through.

00:40:29.421 --> 00:40:34.154
Mr. Paris, full impulse power.

00:40:39.198 --> 00:40:40.222
Shields are down.

00:40:42.301 --> 00:40:44.735
I'm losing power
to the port impulse engine.

00:40:44.770 --> 00:40:46.135
Switching to auxiliary power.

00:40:47.272 --> 00:40:48.830
Hull integrity failing.

00:40:49.942 --> 00:40:51.432
Keep it together, Mr. Paris.

00:41:00.986 --> 00:41:03.113
We've cleared
the event horizon, Captain.

00:41:03.155 --> 00:41:04.952
We've returned to normal space.

00:41:05.057 --> 00:41:08.458
"Sometimes you just have
to punch your way through."

00:41:08.493 --> 00:41:11.223
I'll have to remember that one.

00:41:11.263 --> 00:41:13.060
Set a course.

00:41:13.098 --> 00:41:14.656
I want to be at least
100 million kilometers

00:41:14.700 --> 00:41:17.794
away from the singularity
before we begin repairs.

00:41:30.115 --> 00:41:32.413
There they are... your staff.

00:41:35.053 --> 00:41:38.318
I'll try not to break
any of their noses.

00:41:38.357 --> 00:41:41.155
First order of the day:

00:41:41.193 --> 00:41:44.390
The Captain wants the warp drive
back on line by 1300 hours.

00:41:44.429 --> 00:41:45.828
1300?
That's impossible.

00:41:45.864 --> 00:41:47.889
Then maybe you need
to go break a few noses,

00:41:47.933 --> 00:41:50.868
or at least... bend a few.

00:41:53.005 --> 00:41:54.666
Lieutenant.

00:42:00.612 --> 00:42:02.204
All right.

00:42:02.247 --> 00:42:04.408
Get that isolinear bank
up and running

00:42:04.449 --> 00:42:07.282
and lock down
those plasma relays.

00:42:09.254 --> 00:42:11.119
Please.

00:42:16.428 --> 00:42:19.090
I'm going to be counting on you,
Lieutenant.

00:42:20.399 --> 00:42:24.267
I'm not up-to-date on
the latest Starfleet protocols

00:42:24.303 --> 00:42:27.170
and I know that you're
probably more familiar

00:42:27.205 --> 00:42:29.230
with the quirks
of this warp engine

00:42:29.274 --> 00:42:30.969
than I am right now.

00:42:31.076 --> 00:42:34.876
I hope...
that I can depend on you.

00:42:34.913 --> 00:42:39.145
I assure you, you'll never get
less than my best.

00:42:42.020 --> 00:42:44.113
Lieutenant...

00:42:46.758 --> 00:42:48.487
congratulations.

00:42:48.527 --> 00:42:50.620
Welcome aboard.

00:43:04.076 --> 00:43:06.670
Are you checking up
on your new Chief Engineer?

00:43:06.712 --> 00:43:08.407
Observing.

00:43:08.447 --> 00:43:10.847
And?

00:43:10.882 --> 00:43:12.509
Two crew members have already

00:43:12.551 --> 00:43:14.610
filed complaints
about her promotion,

00:43:14.653 --> 00:43:18.749
and she may be in for a
tough period of adjustment,

00:43:18.790 --> 00:43:20.382
but I think B'Elanna's
going to make

00:43:20.425 --> 00:43:22.416
a fine addition to this crew.

00:43:24.830 --> 00:43:26.923
Our crew.

00:43:26.965 --> 00:43:29.559
Can I ask you a question...

00:43:29.601 --> 00:43:31.535
off the record?

00:43:31.570 --> 00:43:33.765
If things had happened
differently

00:43:33.805 --> 00:43:37.263
and we were on the Maquis
ship now instead of Voyager,

00:43:37.309 --> 00:43:39.243
would you have served under me?

00:43:40.879 --> 00:43:44.315
One of the nice things
about being Captain

00:43:44.349 --> 00:43:47.318
is that you can keep
some things to yourself.

00:43:47.352 --> 00:43:50.515
Sick Bay to Captain Janeway.

00:43:50.555 --> 00:43:51.886
Go ahead.

00:43:51.923 --> 00:43:53.550
Is someone ever going
to come down here

00:43:53.592 --> 00:43:55.492
and fix
my holographic projectors?

00:43:55.527 --> 00:43:57.085
We're a little busy
right now, Doctor,

00:43:57.129 --> 00:43:58.994
but I'll send a crew down
as soon as I can.

00:43:59.097 --> 00:44:02.396
I would appreciate a certain
expediency in the matter.

00:44:02.434 --> 00:44:04.231
Acknowledged.
Janeway out.

00:44:05.470 --> 00:44:07.233
I'm sorry, Lieutenant
but whatever it is,

00:44:07.272 --> 00:44:09.263
you're going to have
to take care of it yourself.

00:44:09.307 --> 00:44:12.504
Oh, it's all right.
It's just a scratch, really.

00:44:12.544 --> 00:44:14.068
You know...

00:44:14.112 --> 00:44:16.740
I like you better this way.
